this time last year we were heading into  the worst wave of the pandemic so far  with hospital admissions in england on  the steep rise towards 4 000 a day  fast forward to last month and here we  can see a similarly sharp increase  the good news is that daily admissions  remain below half what they were  at the peak  but ministers are concerned about a rise  in hospital admissions among older  population groups the over 85 is a  relatively small pool of people but  adjusted for population has been the  worst affected throughout the pandemic  more broadly admission rates are going  up in all adult age groups shown by this  uptick  in december  this is a snapshot of confirmed coveted  patients in hospital in the past three  months increasing evidence is that  omicron is a milder variant of covid but  it spreads incredibly quickly  to see what this means in hospital we  can divide the total number of patients  into three groups the first the dark  line at the top is the people in  hospital for another ailment but test  positive for the virus through routine  testing they now make up about a third  of confirmed covet patients  the more important block the one in the  middle the light blue block is the  number of people in hospital for covert  treatment the most critically ill those  on mechanical ventilators the bottom  line are here  this number has remained pretty stable  so increasing numbers of patients in  hospital for covid but they're not as  ill as in previous waves  general trends are a helpful tool but  it's more of a mixed bag when we break  the data down by regions the rest of  england is estimated to be about 10 days  behind london you can see hospital  admissions starting  rising in the capital from mid december  but and it's a very cautious but there  are the first signs of a slowdown  according to the head of nhs providers  government ministers will be keeping an  eye on this other regions of england are  still on the rise adjusted for  population hospital admissions in the  northwest and the midlands  are shooting up  remember this data won't reflect  household mixing at christmas due to the  lag between catching coved and serious  illness and it will be another week or  two before we see a potential impact of  schools going back  a lot  still hangs  in the balance
 

